: 0.092, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_URL_TERMINATED_PARAGRAPH": {"items": [], "secs": 0.106, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_BFSERIES_HEADING_PARAGRAPH": {"items": [], "secs": 0.082, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_STANDING_ASSUMPTION_ITEMIZE": {"items": [], "secs": 0.124, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_INLINE_SUBSECTION_ASSUMPTION": {"items": [], "secs": 0.141, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_BOLD_ENUM_BLOCK": {"items": [], "secs": 0.093, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_PAR_PROSE_SIGNAL": {"items": [], "secs": 0.077, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_INTERENV_EXAMPLE_PROSE": {"items": [{"raw": "$\\#c\\langle r^3\\rangle$ is, astonishingly, not in OEIS!\\footnote{Update: it is now A259049.} It corresponds to Stanley's case 5 (self-complementary plane partitions), and is non-zero only for even $n$, for a slightly different reason. If we divide the hexagon into six large triangles by drawing the diameters through its vertices, each of these large triangles has $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ small triangles in one orientation and $\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}$ small triangles in another orientation. For $\\langle r^3\\rangle$ we can consider a half-hexagon formed from three of the large triangles: the symmetry will give us the tiling of the other half. This half-hexagon has a surplus of $n$ triangles in one orientation; therefore there must be $n$ triangles along the diameter which pair across into the other half-hexagon. (See figure 2). By the rotational symmetry, these $n$ triangles must be paired up, but if $n$ is odd this is impossible. For even $n$ we have $\\#c\\langle r^3\\rangle(2m) = \\#c\\langle e\\rangle(m)^2$ \\cite{Stanley86}.", "clean": "\\#clangle r^3rangle is, astonishingly, not in OEIS!footnoteUpdate: it is now A259049. It corresponds to Stanley's case 5 (self-complementary plane partitions), and is non-zero only for even n, for a slightly different reason. If we divide the hexagon into six large triangles by drawing the diameters through its vertices, each of these large triangles has fracn(n+1)2 small triangles in one orientation and frac(n-1)n2 small triangles in another orientation. For langle r^3rangle we can consider a half-hexagon formed from three of the large triangles: the symmetry will give us the tiling of the other half. This half-hexagon has a surplus of n triangles in one orientation; therefore there must be n triangles along the diameter which pair across into the other half-hexagon. (See figure 2). By the rotational symmetry, these n triangles must be paired up, but if n is odd this is impossible. For even n we have \\#clangle r^3rangle(2m) = \\#clangle erangle(m)^2 .", "name": null, "type": "example", "span": 8320}], "secs": 0.09, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_POST_ENV_HISTORICAL_REMARK": {"items": [], "secs": 0.093, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_NOTICE_THAT_REMARK": {"items": [], "secs": 0.08, "timeout": false}, "PATTERN_STRUCTURAL_BOUNDARY_PROSE": {"items": [{"raw": "The combinatorics of tilings of a hexagon of integer side-length $n$ by $120^\\circ-60^\\circ$ diamonds of side-length $1$ has a long history, both directly (as a problem of interest in thermodynamic models) and indirectly (through the equivalence to plane partitions \\cite{David89}). Formulae as products of factorials have been conjectured and, one by one, proven for the number of such tilings under each of the symmetries of the hexagon. However, when this note was written the entry for the number of \\textit{distinct} such tilings in the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences (OEIS) consisted of little more than a table for $0 \\le n \\le 4$ and a brief discussion of those values \\cite{A066931}. The aim of this note is to pull together the relevant facts and allow the entry to be improved.", "clean": "The combinatorics of tilings of a hexagon of integer side-length n by 120^circ-60^circ diamonds of side-length 1 has a long history, both directly (as a problem of interest in thermodynamic models) and indirectly (through the equivalence to plane partitions ). Formulae as products of factorials have been conjectured and, one by one, proven for the number of such tilings under each of the symmetries of the hexagon. However, when this note was written the entry for the number of distinct such tilings in the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences (OEIS) consisted of little more than a table for 0 le n le 4 and a brief discussion of those values . The aim of this note is to pull together the relevant facts and allow the entry to be improved.", "name": null, "type": "remark", "span": 13}, {"raw": "At this point, it is worth noting that although there are $16$ subgroups, there are only $10$ conjugacy groups because the $9$ subgroups which contain only reflections each have three choices of axis / axes. Thus there is no disagreement with Stanley's list of 10 symmetries of plane partitions \\cite{Stanley86}.", "clean": "At this point, it is worth noting that although there are 16 subgroups, there are only 10 conjugacy groups because the 9 subgroups which contain only reflections each have three choices of axis / axes. Thus there is no disagreement with Stanley's list of 10 symmetries of plane partitions .", "name": null, "type": "remark", "span": 2691}], "secs": 0.095, "timeout": false}}, "full": [{"raw": "The combinatorics of tilings of a hexagon of integer side-length $n$ by $120^\\circ-60^\\circ$ diamonds of side-length $1$ has a long history, both directly (as a problem of interest in thermodynamic models) and indirectly (through the equivalence to plane partitions \\cite{David89}). Formulae as products of factorials have been conjectured and, one by one, proven for the number of such tilings under each of the symmetries of the hexagon. However, when this note was written the entry for the number of \\textit{distinct} such tilings in the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences (OEIS) consisted of little more than a table for $0 \\le n \\le 4$ and a brief discussion of those values \\cite{A066931}. The aim of this note is to pull together the relevant facts and allow the entry to be improved.", "clean": "The combinatorics of tilings of a hexagon of integer side-length n by 120^circ-60^circ diamonds of side-length 1 has a long history, both directly (as a problem of interest in thermodynamic models) and indirectly (through the equivalence to plane partitions ). Formulae as products of factorials have been conjectured and, one by one, proven for the number of such tilings under each of the symmetries of the hexagon. However, when this note was written the entry for the number of distinct such tilings in the Online Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences (OEIS) consisted of little more than a table for 0 le n le 4 and a brief discussion of those values . The aim of this note is to pull together the relevant facts and allow the entry to be improved.", "name": null, "type": "remark", "span": 13}, {"raw": "At this point, it is worth noting that although there are $16$ subgroups, there are only $10$ conjugacy groups because the $9$ subgroups which contain only reflections each have three choices of axis / axes. Thus there is no disagreement with Stanley's list of 10 symmetries of plane partitions \\cite{Stanley86}.", "clean": "At this point, it is worth noting that although there are 16 subgroups, there are only 10 conjugacy groups because the 9 subgroups which contain only reflections each have three choices of axis / axes. Thus there is no disagreement with Stanley's list of 10 symmetries of plane partitions .", "name": null, "type": "remark", "span": 2691}, {"raw": "$\\#c\\langle r^3\\rangle$ is, astonishingly, not in OEIS!\\footnote{Update: it is now A259049.} It corresponds to Stanley's case 5 (self-complementary plane partitions), and is non-zero only for even $n$, for a slightly different reason. If we divide the hexagon into six large triangles by drawing the diameters through its vertices, each of these large triangles has $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ small triangles in one orientation and $\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}$ small triangles in another orientation. For $\\langle r^3\\rangle$ we can consider a half-hexagon formed from three of the large triangles: the symmetry will give us the tiling of the other half. This half-hexagon has a surplus of $n$ triangles in one orientation; therefore there must be $n$ triangles along the diameter which pair across into the other half-hexagon. (See figure 2). By the rotational symmetry, these $n$ triangles must be paired up, but if $n$ is odd this is impossible. For even $n$ we have $\\#c\\langle r^3\\rangle(2m) = \\#c\\langle e\\rangle(m)^2$ \\cite{Stanley86}.", "clean": "\\#clangle r^3rangle is, astonishingly, not in OEIS!footnoteUpdate: it is now A259049. It corresponds to Stanley's case 5 (self-complementary plane partitions), and is non-zero only for even n, for a slightly different reason. If we divide the hexagon into six large triangles by drawing the diameters through its vertices, each of these large triangles has fracn(n+1)2 small triangles in one orientation and frac(n-1)n2 small triangles in another orientation. For langle r^3rangle we can consider a half-hexagon formed from three of the large triangles: the symmetry will give us the tiling of the other half. This half-hexagon has a surplus of n triangles in one orientation; therefore there must be n triangles along the diameter which pair across into the other half-hexagon. (See figure 2). By the rotational symmetry, these n triangles must be paired up, but if n is odd this is impossible. For even n we have \\#clangle r^3rangle(2m) = \\#clangle erangle(m)^2 .", "name": null, "type": "example", "span": 8320}]}, "algorithm": {"units": ["_EXCLUDED_ENV_RE", "MYPAR_ALGORITHM_RE", "MYPAR_GENERAL_RE", "ORDINAL_METHOD_RE", "REMARK_ENV_RE", "PROCEDURAL_PARA_RE", "ALGORITHM2E_ENV_RE", "NUMBERED_STEP_PROSE_RE", "PSEUDOCODE_ENV_RE", "BOLDITEM_LIST_ENV_RE", "BOLDITEM_ITEM_RE", "SECTION_ALGORITHM_TITLE_RE", "BOLD_HEADING_ENUMERATE_RE", "MDFRAMED_ENV_RE", "CONSTRUCT_ENV_RE"], "uses_spans": false, "f